vidence suggests competition, so the fundamentals stand without adjustment.", "watch": "A strongly favorable national environment for Democrats or an unusually compelling ticket could narrow the margin, though no recent cycle has put North Dakota in play.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "North Dakota remains one of the most Republican states in presidential politics, with a partisan lean of roughly 18 points toward the GOP. The race is an open contest with no incumbent on the ballot, and no candidate-specific polling, expert ratings, or market pricing has emerged to suggest competition. Recent cycles have been uniformly lopsided, with Republican nominees carrying the state by double digits.\n\nThe forecast therefore rests on the state's fundamentals. With a neutral national environment assumed and no evidence of Democratic strength, the Republican nominee is expected to win by a wide margin. The main source of uncertainty is the absence of polling, which keeps the error band wide, but even a strong Democratic year would likely only narrow, not close, the gap in a state this red.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0273, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0558, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0549, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0572, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0505, "margin": -18.0}]}